      "page_title": "Subprocesses: exec vs shell, Pipes & Pitfalls",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What it is",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "==create_subprocess_exec== passes program and args separately, with **no** shell",
            "`create_subprocess_shell(cmd)` runs the command through `/bin/sh`",
            "Untrusted input in a shell cmd needs `shlex.quote`, or it is a {red:shell-injection} risk",
            "Both return a ==Process== object for the running child"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "PIPE = asyncio.subprocess.PIPE",
            "proc = await asyncio.",
            "  create_subprocess_exec(",
            "    'ls', '-l', stdout=PIPE)",
            "print(proc.pid, proc.returncode)"
          ],
          "note": "returncode is None while running"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Under the hood",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Wraps ==loop.subprocess_exec==(protocol_factory, ...)",
            "The factory builds a `SubprocessStreamProtocol`",
            "It exposes `proc.stdin/stdout/stderr` as **streams** (see p.8)",
            "Windows: needs ==ProactorEventLoop== (the default since 3.8)",
            "Unix: a child watcher / __pidfd__ reaps exited children"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
